A||||11||ChadMan||How can I lower my autocross times?||||5||
Z||000000||ChadMan||04-24-2001||08:44 AM||||The bitter taste of defeat has been in my mouth all morning.  Or maybe I forgot to brush my teeth this morning.  Either way, I am wondering how I can lower my times at the autocross and beat at least one MR2 or Miata.<P>My first time was a 36.4xx...not too good (a Z06 Corvette was turning low 29's).  By the day's end, I learned a little bit and was consistently in the low 35's with a best of 35 flat.  I'm thinking...<P>Learn to drive better:  1 to 1.5 sec<BR>Replace worn tie-rod ends and get alignment:  0.5 sec (alignment isn't too far off right now)<BR>Kumho Victoracer tires (next season?):  2 sec<P>Any other ideas?  Also, how much quicker do you think a V6 would be than my Iron Duke?  I guess we'll find out the first time Eric and I race together.<P>Thanks...||||reg||5
Z||000001||thomas_l||04-24-2001||08:56 AM||||Racing tires and practice will get you the most gain. This Sunday I plan to autocross my 87 GT now that it is pretty much safe to drive after a month of weekend "projects" and going a bit over budget  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I have been racing my '00 Lincoln LS and consistently running 5% slower than the other FS cars that have soft, sticky racing rubber.<P>My strategy is to run my 87 GT the rest of this year on street tires to learn how to drive it. As part of my projects I had to replace the rear tires and got some Sumitomo HTR100's which are fairly soft for street tread. This winter I'll probably invest in a spare set of wheels and some Kumhos - provided my engine holds together (a little loose at 166k!)||||reg||10
Z||000002||rogergarrison||04-24-2001||09:38 AM||||Hi chad. Id think as a beginner Id stick with the 4, better wieght distribution. the V6 will be a little more tail happy and not as ez to get the front tires to bite for turning. You didnt look that bad for first time. Who knows im so rusty you might even blow me away next time if I try it. I get a little more carried away in the fun of the moment than competing. Lots to be said about the fun factor smoking your tires sideways thru the curves.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||04-24-2001||12:58 PM||||Drive faster???||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroHarry||04-24-2001||01:18 PM||||Hey pick up the game books for Grand Turismo.<BR>Might sound funny but they have great coverage on turning corner's of different types, ways of handling apex's you might not have heard about.<BR>Im not trying to say anything about you but maybe you might learn something you didnt know before  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Harry<BR>FOCOSEVA<BR>1985 GT 262K+ "TEMPTATION"||||reg||12
Z||000005||Spektyr||04-24-2001||02:12 PM||||How about as soon as you leaving the starting line, just making a turn straight for the finish line?  Damn the cones, full speed ahead!<P>If nothing else you'd get a big laugh...<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Black88GT||04-24-2001||02:20 PM||||Chadman, might want to talk to Jonathan M., slammedfiero on the forum, he sounds like he has the best autoX setup for 84-87 suspensions.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eric||04-24-2001||08:20 PM||||Chadman has an 88 coupe so I don't know how much Slammed Fiero could help him out other than giving him some general advice.<P>I think you did pretty well, Chadman.  I think very very few people who ran faster times than you were first timers like yourself, and that's in any car.  Next time out, you'll convince yourself the only way to drive faster is to drive crazier.  You'll find the limits of your car and make an ass out of yourself.  Hopefully, by the time the first points event rolls around, you'll learn what you can do and where you can do it on the track.  Hang in there, Bubba.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Firestarter_87||04-24-2001||09:09 PM||||Remember that every 100# adds .1 in the 1/4 mi.  I know it isn't apples to apples, but make sure that the tank isn't full, remove any unecessary equipment and if you get real serious take the same rules towards yourself.  I don't recomend trying to race nekid with a helmet though.  It does shave a few ounces, but those dang belt clasps sting. ;~p<P>Firestarter||||reg||1
